place to  measure the progression of flu as it  spreads across a network of people in a  city and recently they have ramped  and combined with other organizations to  create this Seattle coronavirus  assessment network which essentially is  measuring all of these hard to measure  variables to try to quantify the you  know the the nuances of the spread of  coronavirus in in Seattle so I think  that's really cool and this is really  important to have these kinds of  modeling and sensing networks already in  place so I think one of the reasons that  Southeast Asia has been pretty effective  in their efforts to contain kovat 19 is  because years earlier they had the SARS  scare they were really worried that SARS  was gonna do exactly what ko vid 19 is  doing now and so they put in lots of  infrastructure and sensor networks and  surveillance networks because of SARS  because they knew that this this could  happen and so they had that  infrastructure in place and that's  something that you know is absolutely  vital if you really want good effective  kind of responsive control over your  system you need to be measuring more  than just how many people are sick every  day in a city okay that's not enough we  need more detailed measurements in time  okay and you know so I've already shown  you lots about the models we use for  these systems and the models we use to  turn a design control strategies but all  of these parameters the recovery rate  the infection rate all of the parameters  of these models and how they affect  different population groups different  age groups different socio-economic  groups you know from city to city these  parameters are different and under  different social distancing and  different control strategies all of  these parameters are different that's  what we're trying to control but you  need extensive and accurate measurements  to understand what these parameters are  and how effective your control  strategies are so I think that's really  important you absolutely need you know  extensive measurements to get good  models to make effective control  decisions especially since we have time  delays we need our models to be as good  as possible and so we need all of the  information  that we can get our hands on so I've  already talked a lot about time delays  both in this video and in others in my  mind when I think about the spread of  coronavirus and our efforts kind of  within the US and internationally I am  you know constantly thinking about time  delays because as a control theorist I  know that these are some of the  trickiest issues that you ever have to  deal with in a control system is when  there are time delays between what you  measure and and what actually is  happening in the system or between when  you think you've enacted a control  strategy and when it actually becomes  effective or when you think that you  know someone is showing symptoms and  when they actually got infected and  infected others time delays are really  really tricky and so I think that we  absolutely have to be thinking about our  surveillance networks knowing what we  know about time delays and how they  affect robust performance so a lot of  times if you make control decisions and  you have time delays in your system you  can get unstable responses where you're  kind of doing the wrong control at the  wrong time because there's a delay and  your system can go unstable that's a  really big issue and so you know one  strategy when you have these big time  delays is to have good models that  predict where your system is going to be  you make decisions based on that and  then on a slower supervisory time scale  you make corrections based on the  measurements you're taking and those  measurements can improve the model which  improves the kind of aggressiveness with  which you can make fast control  decisions so the measurements are good  for both the modeling and for the  feedback and really are are quite  essential there's already discussions  about software you know to track our  people you know properly socially  distancing to collect that information  you know this is a pretty simple  computer vision problem now actually I  mean this is this is commercial  there's also discussions about whether  or not we should have you know thermal  scans and airports and in train stations  and places so can we measure  does someone have a fever in real time  okay and this brings up a lot of privacy  concerns so you know having an extensive  surveillance network and having  measurements of your system is critical  for good feedback control but there are  big privacy concerns do we want to ramp  up a massive surveillance network that's  something that you know is a real  concern and something that we really  need to think about I mean we all are  carrying you know essentially a big  suite of sensors and it tells us where  we are and how close to people we are  when we go to crowded areas you know  there is a tremendous amount of data  that could be tapped and there are big  societal questions that we have to ask  and answer about how to use that data  responsibly whether we do how much of it  to use things like that those are you  know bigger than any one of us can can  decide okay good so you know I hope I've  kind of convinced you of how important  this measurements and sensors are in  this feedback loop and how we can really  only get the kind of control performance  we need if we have fast accurate sensors  there's a lot of talk now about contact  tracing which basically means if you  have a sick person can you go and trace  all of the people they've been in  contact with and then test them and try  to contain and do a much more isolated  quarantine that way this is also again  happening in lots of other countries  around the world to varying degrees of  effectiveness this is what a lot of  people call boot leather epidemiology or  boots on the ground it takes a lot of  people a lot of resources to go do that  detective work of contact tracing that's  another kind of sensor network that we  can deploy at a finer more granular  level okay good  so I hope I've given you some some  feeling for how hard of a problem this  is there are you know ethical societal  concerns there are technological  concerns there are practical concerns  about the time delays that your sensors  will inevitably have in this  control architecture but I do firmly  believe that if you want to do a good  job you need to be extensively measuring  your system all right thank you


